WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.540
There are elements of this that exist,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.131
not at the human level,

00:00:08.131 --> 00:00:10.747
but at the animal-trial mice level.

00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:12.560
Hi, I'm Dr. Ayanna Howard.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.730
I'm the dean of engineering
at The Ohio State University.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.540
I was chair of interactive
computing at Georgia Tech.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.130
I was a robotics researcher at NASA's JPL.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.800
Today, we're going to look
at robotics scenes in movies

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.833
and judge how real they are.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.200
So, that actually exists now.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.630
Primarily it's in the VR/AR space,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.920
which requires you to usually have

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.530
some type of augmented goggles or a device

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.900
to see the 3D rendition in the free space

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.283
and for you to move around it.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.870
Believe it or not, jet packs do exist.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.257
Not as much control as
the actual Iron Man,

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:59.420
but, you know, has some vertical lift.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.280
In the healthcare medical space,

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.550
there are exoskeletons that provide you,

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.730
I would say, superhuman
ability or capability.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:10.890
And so pieces of the
Iron Man suit do exist.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.670
They aren't as advanced,
but they all exist.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.350
So I would say an 8.5.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.266
Another three or four years,
it would probably be a 10.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.980
Hello. I am Baymax,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.290
your personal healthcare companion.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.800
Oh, it's such a cute movie.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.250
So unrealistic.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.350
The only thing that exists is
the fact that you have people

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.300
and a robot of form factor.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.900
Everything else is just imaginary.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.270
It's what we would like in our life.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.860
But we're kind of far

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.403
from even the components of them existing.

00:01:44.753 --> 00:01:45.720
Vinyl?

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.950
Yeah. Going for a nonthreatening
huggable kind of thing.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.770
So, soft robotics does exist.

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.927
Soft-body robotics is where you have

00:01:53.927 --> 00:01:55.940
material that's compliant.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.950
I always think of it as it's squishy.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.030
It actually can apply some forces.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.640
Usually the focus is on movement.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.500
They are considered safe.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.980
It means you can have robots
working next to people,

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.290
and the soft robot wouldn't hurt a person

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:13.190
if by some chance the robot
and the human collided.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:14.690
So really it's about safety.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.220
They aren't really designed to do things

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.360
like walk like a human.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.580
Baymax: You are not
allergic to bacitracin.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.010
You do have a mild allergy to peanuts.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.480
I will say, in the
medical-robotics domain,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.800
as related to this video clip,

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.180
there are elements that exist.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.725
For example, there are systems that

00:02:34.725 --> 00:02:37.233
can do diagnosis based on data.

00:02:37.233 --> 00:02:41.990
They can have an image that
looks for things such as tumors.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.920
And sometimes in some
cases at more accuracy

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.320
or higher accuracy than,
say, a untrained radiologist.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.650
So those kind of things do exist.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.240
They're usually not coupled
to a moving platform.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.756
I would probably rate it about a 2.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:01.750
You can now access the CPU.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.530
Central processing unit
is what CPU stands for.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.780
The CPU is basically the chip that powers,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.623
in this case, the mind of the robot.

00:03:11.288 --> 00:03:12.478
Pull.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.380
Robot vision is based on cameras.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.230
The same kind of cameras
you have in your smartphone

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.010
are the same kind of cameras

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.910
that you have in robot platforms.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.220
A lot of computer vision
algorithms use methods

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.740
in deep learning to identify
objects, identify people,

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.110
even identify facial
expressions of people.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.360
The red tint was for

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.485
entertainment purposes only.

00:03:41.125 --> 00:03:43.490
Rebooting is a thing, it's a reality.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.200
Rebooting includes
refreshing your computer.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.620
You can do that with a robotic system.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.553
It wipes out all its programming.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.790
Robots like Sophia are not
as emotive as a human face.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.790
And the reason is, is that the muscles

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.550
that we have in our face

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.970
and the kind of nuances
that we can exhibit

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:10.540
in terms of cheeks, in
our nose, in our eyes,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.540
we just don't have the
technology yet at that level.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.670
I would rate the first part as a 1.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.486
I would rate the second
part closer to a 5.

00:04:28.942 --> 00:04:32.950
There's a lot of hurdles, even
creating a transformer robot.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:36.330
There is research in robotics
called origami robotics.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.200
So they can fold themselves up.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.230
They can change their locomotion style.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.850
They're typically very, very small.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.560
My lab actually created
a transformer robot,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.050
but it was really, really small.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:46.983
Some of the issues,

00:04:46.983 --> 00:04:49.900
it really is about stability and control.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.610
The fact is, is the larger
you create the form factor,

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.550
the more you have to think
about things like gravity.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:57.900
You have to think about weight.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.380
How do you ensure that
they don't fall over?

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:03.580
We do not have sufficient actuation

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.438
to lift the weight that would be required

00:05:07.438 --> 00:05:09.430
to create a transformer robot.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:13.003
I would rate this as a 2.5.

00:05:13.003 --> 00:05:14.340
I probably would rate it as a 1,

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.830
but I have an affinity
for transformer robots.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.772
What I really need is
a droid who understands

00:05:18.772 --> 00:05:20.952
the binary language of
moisture evaporators.

00:05:20.952 --> 00:05:21.820
Evaporators!

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.590
Sir, my first job was
programming binary load lifters.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.130
I always loved how they
showcase robots in "Star Wars,"

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:33.850
because they had all of
the types of interactions

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:36.380
we expect between human and human,

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:38.753
and they encode it in a robot form factor.

00:05:38.753 --> 00:05:41.503
[R2-D2 whines]

00:05:41.503 --> 00:05:44.736
[R2-D2 beeps]

00:05:46.424 --> 00:05:48.290
There is actually a number of studies

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:51.055
that have looked at the
form factor of robotics.

00:05:51.055 --> 00:05:52.968
Do you have to have a humanoid shape

00:05:52.968 --> 00:05:55.940
in order to enhance the
interaction with people?

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:59.060
Most studies show that you don't.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:00.600
It just has to be able to function

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.390
and behave and move in
our human environment.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.640
R2-D2, if you think about the
form factor and the shape,

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.530
that is not what evokes
our human response,

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.970
or our human compassion
or empathy for the robot.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.310
What makes R2-D2 so endearing

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.350
is a combination of the movement,

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:23.890
so the movements that go on in
terms of doing little dances,

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.740
for example, turning in place;

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.350
the other is the sounds that are used.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:30.650
They're very intentional.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:33.430
If you notice, they're
typically high-pitched.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.472
We associate high-pitched sounds
with certain demographics,

00:06:36.472 --> 00:06:39.450
like birds chirping, babies laughing.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.615
And so what's going on
is that it's evoking

00:06:42.615 --> 00:06:46.291
that response of the
cuteness of the sounds

00:06:46.291 --> 00:06:47.610
coupled with the movement.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.670
It's not the form factor,
it's the movements

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:55.090
and the language that really
taps into our human emotions.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.748
So I would actually put this at a 6.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.500
So, there is a field in robotics

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.570
called multiagent collaboration

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:11.500
as well as this area of
collaborative robotics.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:12.610
And in that case,

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:17.230
there is research that has
shown on the order of hundreds

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.320
of robots that collaborate together.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.360
There's different approaches.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.420
Some of it is a centralized approach.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:25.770
So there might be a leader
that kind of gives commands

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.660
and communicates with all of the team.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:28.520
And then there's also

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.060
what's called distributed collaboration

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.320
or distributed control, where every agent,

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:34.640
every robot has its own objective function

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:36.460
and kind of communicates as in a team.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:39.990
As an example, there
are robot soccer teams.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.100
That's collaborative.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.200
They play together,
they interact together.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.820
Small robots like this are possible.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:58.290
In fact, there are robots
about this size that can fly.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.490
You know, the size of a bird,

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:02.130
of a butterfly, things like that.

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.450
Of course, they don't
have as much functionality

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:07.600
as these robots or as much
intelligence as these robots.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.440
There's even a field of robotics

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.570
where the robots are even
smaller. Nanorobotics.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.095
For example, there's a robot

00:08:15.095 --> 00:08:18.311
that is a pill that you swallow

00:08:18.311 --> 00:08:22.140
that can actually do some of
the imaging in your intestines.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.807
That's a whole field that requires

00:08:24.807 --> 00:08:27.130
different types of tools,
different types of materials.

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:28.530
And in that space,

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.920
you are actually doing things
at the microscope level.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.270
You're actually using things

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.540
like tweezers to put things together.

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:38.410
I would rate this probably at a 3.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.750
This movie actually defies physics.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:53.500
Just [laughs] some fundamental
things about the interface

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.567
and the interaction of bodies and water

00:08:56.567 --> 00:08:59.213
that just didn't correlate in this movie.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:01.353
Cooper: Come on!

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:08.180
So, versatility is a desired objective,

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:12.650
but we typically do not focus on it,

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:15.360
because the more versatile you are,

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.310
the more likely you'll have failures.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:21.360
Safety and minimizing failures to zero

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.160
is much more important
than having versatility.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:27.880
So for space robotics, a lot of the things

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.250
that have to be thought about
are about survivability.

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:35.470
They have to be able to
withstand the radiation of space,

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:37.090
the environmental constraints,

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:40.300
the extreme temperatures
from basically cold.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:42.625
A lot of our space robotics,

00:09:42.625 --> 00:09:45.225
in terms of the processing power,

00:09:45.225 --> 00:09:47.860
is not even as advanced as, say,

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.490
things on the latest smartphone,

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:54.449
because they cannot withstand the severity

00:09:54.449 --> 00:09:56.670
of the environment in
which they're going into.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.097
I would rate this a 1.

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.580
There are elements of this that exist,

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:06.163
not at the human level,

00:10:06.163 --> 00:10:08.746
but at the animal-trial mice level.

00:10:08.746 --> 00:10:11.910
Looking at the spine and tapping
into the electric signals

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.370
and trying to use that
to control something.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:17.080
The closest that exists in the domain

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.910
are really two areas of robotics research.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:22.950
So, one is basically
using muscle activation.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:24.263
In that case, what they do

00:10:24.263 --> 00:10:26.450
is that they pair up your muscles,

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:27.640
usually in your chest,

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:30.080
and they pair it up with
electronics to control the arm.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:31.500
And so that's one example.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.430
The other is, is that there's research

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:36.360
where they do direct
implants into the brain

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.160
so that they can extract signals

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.560
from the brain to control robotic arms.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.350
I will tell you, both
of them are invasive.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.288
They aren't as simple as
just strapping on something

00:10:46.288 --> 00:10:48.280
and automatically you
can control robotics.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.880
Both of these kinds of research paths

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:52.915
require intense surgery.

00:10:52.915 --> 00:10:55.170
Doc Ock: These smart arms are controlled

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.530
by my brain through a neural link.

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:00.490
Nanowires feed directly
into my cerebellum,

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.470
allowing me to use these arms.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:05.360
The closest of controlling
robots with your thoughts

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.068
is looking at some research

00:11:07.068 --> 00:11:10.070
that uses EEG devices, or even EMG.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:12.930
So actually looking at muscle activation.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:16.200
The reality is the resolution
that we can extract

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:20.240
in terms of bits of information
is still very limited.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.550
It means that if you're
trying to control a robot arm,

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.140
the most you can kind of think
about is, like, a joystick.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.170
I can go up, I can go
down, left, and right,

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:32.440
but it's very difficult
to do that intentionally

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.800
over and over and over again

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:35.710
with the resolution that you would need

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:40.110
to fully control a robot arm,
especially multiple arms.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.343
I would actually rate this as a 4.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:51.060
This case, they did design it
based on an actual platform

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:54.100
with the way that it moves,
the way that it navigates.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:05.910
The sensor that was shown,
which was most likely a lidar,

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.570
does exist in a small platform.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:08.978
So lidars, for example,

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:12.211
are in some of the self-driving cars.

00:12:12.211 --> 00:12:15.420
They are on a number of mobility platforms

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.087
in the warehouse environment.

00:12:17.087 --> 00:12:19.053
And so what they do is that they create

00:12:19.053 --> 00:12:21.486
a 3D point cloud of the environment,

00:12:21.486 --> 00:12:26.195
and they are fairly robust to
things like lighting issues

00:12:26.195 --> 00:12:29.060
and convex and concave objects.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:31.400
And they are now at a
small enough footprint

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:35.383
that they can fit into the
torso of the robot that we saw.

00:12:41.637 --> 00:12:44.310
Having a robot plug into
an electronic device

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:47.430
and magically be able to hack the code

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.840
and override any passwords

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:53.290
and encryption scheme
is really not feasible.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:04.185
The energy requirements
for a robotic system

00:13:04.185 --> 00:13:07.129
to continuously activate, or actuate,

00:13:07.129 --> 00:13:10.490
to do the processing in
terms of the computation,

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:14.240
to do the sensing, solar
power would not provide

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.763
the longevity that's required

00:13:16.763 --> 00:13:18.910
to actually function for more
than a couple of minutes.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:20.890
Traditionally, you
wouldn't use solar power,

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:23.410
at least for this application.

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:27.016
I would probably rate this at about a 3.

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.127
Detective, what are you doing?

00:13:30.127 --> 00:13:31.343
Well, you said they've all been programmed

00:13:31.343 --> 00:13:32.251
with the three laws.

00:13:32.251 --> 00:13:34.090
So that means we have 1,000 robots

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:35.670
that will not try to protect themselves

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.750
if it violates a direct
order from a human.

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:41.540
Isaac Asimov created these
three laws of robotics.

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:44.720
One was thou shall not harm a human.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:47.330
Two was thou shall obey a human,

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:49.160
as long as it doesn't harm a human.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:53.590
Basically, the concept is that
robots are here to help us,

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:56.040
and therefore they should never be

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:58.190
in a situation where they're not.

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.530
But the fact is that's
never going to happen.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:02.930
If you think about a very simple device,

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:04.830
such as a self-driving car,

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:07.210
which is a robot if it's autonomous,

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:10.450
what do you do if you have your driver

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:12.910
and you are about to crash into something?

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:16.160
Either you might harm your driver,

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.500
you might harm the other,
what are you supposed to do?

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:23.280
And so really you can't actually
follow any of these rules

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.022
if you are in the human world.

00:14:29.018 --> 00:14:31.930
It is not possible to hurl a human

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:35.950
exactly the way that the
robot hurled Will Smith.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:40.600
But the reason why robots
in industrial settings

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.530
typically do not work with humans,

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:45.853
if a robot is moving and
a human is in the way,

00:14:45.853 --> 00:14:47.760
the human will be hurled.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.100
We are soft bodies that don't do well

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:53.503
when we interact with metal
that's moving that fast.

00:14:55.147 --> 00:14:56.703
What am I?

00:14:57.578 --> 00:14:59.100
Robots: Can I help you, sir?

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.980
Let's take away the intelligence.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:03.500
If you look at the
actuation and the mobility,

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:04.430
we do have something.

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:08.340
Boston Dynamics with Petman
actually does parkour,

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:12.440
does flips, does turns
in midair, gymnastics.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:14.270
That platform does exist.

00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:16.443
Now, of course, power requirements.

00:15:16.443 --> 00:15:18.030
It doesn't last that long.

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:19.710
It won't survive in the real world

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.502
for hours like we would need,

00:15:21.502 --> 00:15:24.585
but the actuation does exist.

00:15:24.585 --> 00:15:26.810
The intelligence part, though,

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:29.257
is not coupled with the movement part.

00:15:29.257 --> 00:15:31.241
I would rate it as a 3.

00:15:31.241 --> 00:15:32.549
Why don't we start with you telling me

00:15:32.549 --> 00:15:34.542
something about yourself?

00:15:34.542 --> 00:15:37.760
Well, you already know my name,

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:41.210
and you can see that I'm a machine.

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:44.260
The Turing test was a
test that was created

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.210
to really try to figure out,

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:49.010
how do you know when a
computer is intelligent?

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.220
And so the concept is that

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:54.590
if you had an individual
that asked questions

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:56.998
of a computer and of a person,

00:15:56.998 --> 00:16:00.298
if the person who was asking the questions

00:16:00.298 --> 00:16:01.723
could not tell the difference

00:16:01.723 --> 00:16:06.140
between computer and
person, i.e., mislabeled,

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:09.140
then the computer had
passed the Turing test.

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:11.380
The problem, of course,
is that it's the observer

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:14.370
who is saying, "Oh, I
can't tell the difference."

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:17.020
So the Turing test has been,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:19.628
quote, unquote, "beaten" all of the time,

00:16:19.628 --> 00:16:23.760
because it's based on the
observer point of view

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.210
and is based on the
questions that you ask.

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:28.427
When did you learn how to speak, Ava?

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:32.375
I always knew how to speak.

00:16:32.375 --> 00:16:35.174
And that's strange, isn't it?

00:16:35.174 --> 00:16:36.560
You know, a lot of times we're thinking

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:38.080
about a language model.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.940
It takes hours and hours, and
sometimes days, depending,

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:46.350
in order to come up with the
ability to have a conversation.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.590
And so the fact that she very easily

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:53.180
on the order of milliseconds
could acquire new knowledge,

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:56.834
could actually understand new nuances

00:16:56.834 --> 00:16:58.915
to the questions that were asked,

00:16:58.915 --> 00:17:02.448
is actually not a realistic aspect

00:17:02.448 --> 00:17:04.523
of our current state of art.

00:17:04.523 --> 00:17:07.425
I would rate this as a 2.5.

00:17:07.425 --> 00:17:10.602
Both my favorite and the
best depiction, I would say,

00:17:10.602 --> 00:17:13.740
is the "Iron Man" video clip.

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:16.377
If you enjoyed this video,
click the link above.

